{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01VwA4RaZp1jtoTDTWbwWTbn","type":"tool_result","content":"14\t//! To get bit-for-bit identical results even for exotic inputs (infinities and\n15\t//! NaNs), the arithmetic goes through small helpers that reproduce the exact\n16\t//! NaN-propagation rules of the scalar SSE instructions the C compiler emits\n17\t//! (`addss`/`subss`/`mulss`/`divss`): if the first source operand is a NaN the\n18\t//! result is that NaN made quiet, otherwise if the second source operand is a\n19\t//! NaN the result is that NaN made quiet. Relying on plain `+`/`-`/`*` would\n20\t//! leave the choice of operand order (and hence the NaN sign/payload that\n21\t//! survives) up to LLVM's canonicalisation.\n22\t\n23\t#![allow(clippy::missing_safety_doc)]\n24\t\n25\tuse std::ffi::c_float;\n26\t\n27\tunsafe extern \"C\" {\n28\t    /// `float fmodf(float, float)` from libm, exactly as used by the C source.\n29\t    safe fn fmodf(x: c_float, y: c_float) -> c_float;\n30\t}\n31\t\n32\t/// Set the \"quiet\" bit of a NaN, as every SSE arithmetic instruction does when\n33\t/// it forwards a source NaN to its result.\n34\t#[inline]\n35\tfn quiet(v: c_float) -> c_float {\n36\t    c_float::from_bits(v.to_bits() | 0x0040_0000)\n37\t}\n38\t\n39\t/// SSE scalar NaN-operand selection: `src1` wins over `src2`.\n40\t#[inline]\n41\tfn nan_result(src1: c_float, src2: c_float) -> Option<c_float> {\n42\t    if src1.is_nan() {\n43\t        Some(quiet(src1))\n44\t    } else if src2.is_nan() {\n45\t        Some(quiet(src2))\n46\t    } else {\n47\t        None\n48\t    }\n49\t}\n50\t\n51\t/// `addss src1, src2`\n52\t#[inline]\n53\tfn add_ss(src1: c_float, src2: c_float) -> c_float {"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28b3df08-e114-40c6-835f-7a4bcdb69832","uuid":"2df9ae34-0fec-4d2c-b61f-1e6a4b9e9db4","timestamp":"2026-08-26T22:43:21.951Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest